    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Guides",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Generator Size for Central Air",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/what-size-generator-runs-central-air-conditioning/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What size generator do I need to run central air conditioning?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For a three-ton unit alongside household essentials, 14,000 to 18,000 watts, which means a standby installation. The driver is the start-up surge of 9,000 to 12,000 watts rather than the 3,000 to 3,800 running draw. A soft starter can reduce that requirement substantially.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can a portable generator run central air?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Usually not without help. Most portables top out around 9,500 to 12,000 watts, and a three-ton air conditioner can surge to 12,000 on its own before any other load. Fitting a soft starter to the condenser often brings the surge down enough that a large portable becomes viable.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is a soft starter and does it help?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A device fitted to the air conditioner that ramps the compressor up gradually rather than applying full voltage instantly. It commonly cuts a three-ton unit’s starting draw from 9,000-12,000 watts to around 3,000-5,000, frequently moving you from needing a standby unit to a large portable.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Will a 10000 watt generator run central air conditioning?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A 10,000 watt generator may start a smaller two-ton unit with little else running, but it is marginal for a three-ton system, which can surge to 12,000 watts alone. With a soft starter fitted it becomes realistic, and with other household loads connected you would want more headroom.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How many watts does a 3 ton AC use?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Roughly 3,000 to 3,800 watts running, and 9,000 to 12,000 watts at start-up. The gap between those two figures is what drives generator sizing. Check the LRA figure on your condenser data plate and multiply by voltage for a conservative starting number specific to your unit.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is it cheaper to fit a soft starter or buy a bigger generator?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Usually the soft starter, often by a wide margin. The device and its installation are typically a fraction of the difference between a large portable and an installed standby system, and it can move a household from needing 18,000 watts to needing around 9,500.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do inverter mini-splits need less generator capacity than central AC?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Considerably less. An inverter mini-split ramps its compressor up electronically rather than starting it across the line, so it has very little start-up surge. A household on mini-splits can often cool one or two zones from a mid-size portable, which is not possible with a conventional condenser of similar capacity.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why do my lights dim when the AC starts on generator power?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "That is voltage sag, and it means the generator is close to its limit for that surge. It stresses the machine and brownout conditions are hard on motor windings and electronics. Shed other loads, fit a soft starter, or move up a size rather than tolerating it.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Alex Rivers" },
      datePublished: "2026-09-01",
      dateModified: "2026-09-01",
